ted around 1455. Yet the true significance of the press lay not in this single masterpiece but in its capacity for reproducibility. Within fifty years of Gutenberg's invention, an estimated twenty to twenty-five million volumes had been printed across Europe\u2014a quantity that dwarfed the output of the previous century of manual scribal labor.\n\n## The Democratization of Knowledge\n\nBefore the printing press, books were extraordinarily rare and expensive. They had been produced by hand, often in monasteries, requiring hours or even months to copy a single volume. This scarcity meant that literacy was largely confined to the clergy and the wealthy, and that knowledge was concentrated in a few great libraries.\n\nThe printing press shattered this scarcity. The cost of books fell dramatically, and their availability"}